   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public officers law, in relation to the dates by which the\ngovernor may make proclamation of a special election to fill certain offices",
    "basePrintNo": "S7227",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7227-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §42, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Public Officers Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7227\n\nSPONSOR: MYRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public officers law, in relation to the dates by\nwhich the governor may make proclamation of a special election to fill\ncertain offices\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require that the governor call a special election within 10 days of a\nvacancy in an elective office; to shorten the time frame for holding\nsuch special election for all offices except representative in congress;\nand to provide a deadline after which a special election will not be\nheld.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends subdivision 3 of Public Officers Law § 42\nto require that the governor shall issue a proclamation for a special\nelection within 10 days of the occurrence of a vacancy in elective\noffice. Subdivision 3 also shortens the time frame for holding such\nspecial election for all offices, except representative in congress, to\nnot less than 40 days nor more than 50 days after the proclamation is\nissued.\n\nSection one also amends subdivision 4 of Public Officers Law § 42 to\nprovide that a special election shall be held to fill a vacancy in other\nelective offices, in addition to the office of state senator or member\nof the assembly, only if the vacancy occurs before the first day of\nApril of the last year of the term.\n\nSection two sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nVacancies in elective office occur on a regular basis but never more so\nthan when public officials run for different offices and commence the\nterm in the new office on January 1\" of each year. This bill attempts to\nshorten the time that an office remains vacant by mandating that the\ngovernor call a special election to fill such vacancies and requiring\nthe special election to occur sooner than current law requires.\n\nThe bill also expands the deadline of March 31' in the last year of a\nterm of office, after which a special election may not occur, to all\nelective offices for which a governor must issue a proclamation. After\nthis deadline all vacancies must be filled at the general election. This\nincreased uniformity should save resources for the counties, the City of\nNew York, and the boards of elections.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
